  "notes": "Built to the user's description and inspiration photo, not a specific product. A fitted wardrobe wall, wall to wall and floor to ceiling, for the short wall of Zimmer 13.0 on the bathroom side: side columns with a tall and an upper door each, three top cabinets above the bed, push-to-open doors with mouldings, and 3 cm fillers against the walls. Between the columns, a bed alcove lined in walnut: 177 cm wide, 205 cm high and 57 cm deep, with a book-matched back panel, rounded top corners, and a sconce and a niche in each side. Bed 160 × 200 (170 cm wide) fits with 3.5 cm to spare on each side, its headboard against the back panel; its own side clearance then reaches the cheeks. The width, 292 cm, is the plan value of checklist M04, not yet measured. After measuring, rebuild the items with `npx tsx tools/models/wardrobe-wall-bed-alcove.ts <width>` (it writes all three versions): the side columns take up the difference. No clearance zone: zones always cover a whole side, and one in front would cover the bed. The column doors need about 51 cm in front of them; with the bed in the alcove, its side clearance covers that. Other versions: wardrobe-wall-bed-alcove-r5 (corners with a 5 cm radius) and wardrobe-wall-bed-alcove-low (a lower alcove under taller top cabinets), both made for headboard-box-alcove-177. That box fits this alcove's width too, but at 125 cm it covers the niches and runs into the sconces (their shades hang at 103–118 cm, 20–30 cm in front of the back panel). Colorways (fronts / lining / box): white & walnut, the default (lacquer.white / wood.walnut-natural / lacquer.white); all white (lacquer.white everywhere); all walnut (wood.walnut-natural everywhere); chocolate & walnut (lacquer.chocolate / wood.walnut-natural / lacquer.chocolate); light blue & oak (lacquer.light-blue / wood.oak-natural / lacquer.light-blue); off-white & smoked oak (lacquer.off-white / wood.oak-smoked / wood.oak-smoked). Every finish slot takes lacquer, paint and wood, so any other combination works too, including one colour for everything."
